  "promptText": "Build a complete, playable 3D mini-game entirely inside a single web page using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. You may use Three.js from a CDN, but do not require any build tools, frameworks, servers, or external assets that need to be downloaded manually.\n\n### Game: \"Tiny 3D Island\"\n\nCreate a polished low-poly 3D island exploration game viewed from a third-person camera.\n\nThe scene must contain:\n\n* A small floating island with realistic terrain elevation, grass, dirt, rocks, and a surrounding ocean.\n* A small stylized wooden house with walls, roof, door, windows, chimney, and a small porch.\n* At least 5 trees with trunks, branches, and foliage.\n* Rocks, flowers, grass patches, and other environmental details distributed naturally around the island.\n* A sun, sky gradient, clouds, atmospheric fog, and soft shadows.\n* Water that has animated waves and subtle reflections.\n* A character that the player controls with WASD / arrow keys.\n* Smooth third-person camera movement following the character.\n* Character idle, walking, and running animations created procedurally if no external character model is used.\n* Collision detection so the player cannot walk through the house, trees, rocks, or outside the island.\n* A simple day/night cycle.\n* Ambient environmental animation: moving clouds, swaying trees/grass, animated water, and subtle floating particles.\n\n### Gameplay\n\nThe player starts outside the house.\n\nAdd 5 glowing crystals around the island that the player must collect.\n\nWhen the player gets close to a crystal, it should float, rotate, glow, and disappear when collected.\n\nDisplay:\n\nCRYSTALS: 0 / 5\n\nAfter collecting all 5 crystals, show a polished \"Island Complete!\" overlay with a restart button.\n\nAdd a small compass/minimap in the corner showing the player's approximate position and the locations of remaining crystals.\n\n### Interaction\n\nAdd:\n\n* E to enter the house.\n* E to open/close the door.\n* A simple interior containing a table, chair, bed, lamp, and window.\n* When inside, smoothly transition the camera rather than teleporting it.\n* Press ESC to return to the normal exploration view.\n* Mouse drag to rotate the camera.\n* Mouse wheel to zoom.\n* Shift to sprint.\n* Space to jump.\n\n### Visual quality\n\nMake it look like a polished indie game rather than a technical Three.js demo.\n\nUse:\n\n* Consistent low-poly art direction.\n* Good proportions.\n* Depth and layering.\n* Atmospheric perspective.\n* Shadows.\n* Ambient lighting.\n* Emissive materials for crystals and lamps.\n* Smooth camera interpolation.\n* Carefully chosen colors.\n* Procedural geometry where appropriate.\n\nDo not create everything as primitive cubes placed randomly. Construct recognizable objects from multiple meshes and give the environment intentional composition.\n\n### UI\n\nCreate a minimal game HUD:\n\nTop-left:\nCRYSTALS: 0 / 5\n\nBottom-left:\nWASD Move · Shift Sprint · Space Jump · E Interact\n\nTop-right:\nA small compass/minimap.\n\nAdd a small settings button that opens a panel containing:\n\n* Shadows ON/OFF\n* Quality: Low / Medium / High\n* Day/Night slider\n* Reset Game\n\n### Technical requirements\n\nEverything must run by opening the HTML file directly in a browser.\n\nDo not use React, Vue, Vite, npm, TypeScript, or a backend.\n\nKeep the implementation in one self-contained HTML file.\n\nUse Three.js from a CDN if needed.\n\nHandle browser resizing correctly.\n\nUse requestAnimationFrame for the game loop.\n\nAvoid unnecessary dependencies.\n\nOrganize the JavaScript into clear sections/classes for:\n\n* Scene\n* Player\n* Camera\n* Environment\n* Collectibles\n* Collision\n* Input\n* Game state\n* UI\n\nInclude comments explaining important systems.\n\n### Performance\n\nThe game should remain reasonably smooth on a normal modern laptop.\n\nUse instancing or shared geometries/materials where appropriate.\n\nDo not create thousands of unnecessary individual meshes.\n\n### Final requirement\n\nDo not give me an explanation of how to build it.\n\nActually implement the complete game and return the finished HTML code.\n\nThe first screen should immediately show the playable 3D island.\n\nMake reasonable design decisions yourself instead of asking questions."
